---
/*
  SetSheet — make a curated listen, or change one (plan 40 §3, plan 42 · §4.D.2).

  ⚠ THIS REPLACES PLAIN SERVER-RENDERED FORMS, AND THE ARGUMENT THEY LOST IS
  WORTH KEEPING. `sets.astro` used to render every set as an open `<form>` on the
  list, with no client script at all, and defended it in a comment:

    "Every other room in the workshop is JS-driven — sheets, drawers, panels —
     because they edit objects with a dozen fields, bulk selection and live
     preview. A set has FIVE fields and there will be about seven of them.
     Astro's actions accept a form POST natively, so this page is server-
     rendered, works with script disabled, cannot get its optimistic state out
     of step with the database, and is about a tenth of the code a sheet would
     be."

  THREE THINGS WERE WRONG WITH IT, and the first is the one that matters:

   1. ⚠ THE PAGE WAS THE COMMIT UNIT, SO IT COULD LOSE WORK. Every card being a
      live form meant a reorder, a delete, or a Save on ANY card posted and
      reloaded — silently discarding unsaved text in every other card. The
      Library has the same hazard and GUARDED it on 2026-08-12 (`library.ts`,
      a confirm naming the other dirty rows); sets shipped unguarded three days
      later. A sheet has no other cards to lose.
   2. The count was wrong on the day it was written — the card rendered SIX
      controls, and the comment's own escape clause was "if a set ever grows a
      sixth field… this becomes a sheet like its neighbours."
   3. "A tenth of the code" does not survive measurement: `GoalSheet.astro` is
      126 lines for five fields.

  ⚠ AND THE NO-JS HALF IS A REAL ARGUMENT THAT LOSES ON A NARROWER POINT. The
  Observatory is one authenticated person on their own devices; every other room
  already needs script to create anything; and the benefit was bought at the
  price of a page that cannot hold two edits at once. Michael's call, 2026-08-17.
  See the ADR.

  SHAPED AFTER `GoalSheet`, deliberately and to the letter: `drawer-dialog
  max-w-md`, `SheetHeader`, `SheetError`, a footer of Delete · Cancel · Save.
  Six fields is a short record, so the footer IS the end of the object — the
  boundary `scripts/sheet.ts` states.

  ⚠ ONE SHEET, MANY SETS — so it fills on open rather than server-rendering its
  values, which is `TaskSheet`'s shape and not `GoalSheet`'s. A goal page holds
  exactly one goal and can therefore render the form filled; this room lists
  seven, and mounting seven sheets to avoid a prefill step is how you get seven
  copies of a form to keep in step. The row carries its own JSON and
  `set-sheet.ts` reads it.

  ⚠ THE ROW SERIALISES THE COLUMNS, not what it happens to be displaying — the
  rule `TaskSheet` states for the same reason. A list shows a title and a status
  chip; the sheet needs the playlist, the quote and the description too, and
  deriving those from what is on screen is how a form and a row come to disagree.
*/
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';
import MiniEditor from './MiniEditor.astro';
import SheetHeader from './SheetHeader.astro';
import SheetError from './SheetError.astro';

interface Props {
  /** Published quotes, for the epigraph picker. */
  quotes: { id: string; label: string }[];
}
const { quotes } = Astro.props;
---

<dialog id="set-sheet" class="drawer-dialog w-full max-w-md" aria-labelledby="set-sheet-title">
  <form id="set-form" class="flex h-full flex-col overflow-hidden font-sans">
    <SheetHeader titleId="set-sheet-title" title="New set" />

    <SheetError id="set-sheet-error" />

    <div class="min-h-0 flex-1 overflow-y-auto overscroll-contain px-6 py-5">
      <label class="f">
        <span class="f__k">Title <em>required</em></span>
        <input type="text" name="title" required maxlength="300" placeholder="If nothing else, I love you" />
        <span class="f__h">An utterance, not a label — it is the whole of the index on the music page.</span>
      </label>

      <label class="f mt-4">
        <span class="f__k">Playlist <em>required</em></span>
        <input type="url" name="playlist_url" required placeholder="https://open.spotify.com/playlist/…" />
        <span class="f__h">Any Spotify playlist link — the <code>?si=</code> token is stripped on save.</span>
      </label>

      {
        /* ⚠ STILL A `<select>`, AND IT IS THE ONE THING THIS SHEET DOES NOT FIX
          (plan 42 · §4.D.4). The right answer is `FragmentBrowser` in a third
          mode — published quotes, single-select, with the create bar retargeted
          — and it is a bigger piece than the rest of this file. Carried across
          unchanged rather than quietly improved, so the finding stays open and
          visible instead of looking done.

          What DID change: it is rendered ONCE here instead of once per card
          plus once in the New form. Seven sets × 77 quotes was 616 `<option>`
          elements for a control only ever open once — the same O(n²) shape
          plan 38 · §1.2 deleted from the Library. */
      }
      <label class="f mt-4">
        <span class="f__k">Quote <span>— optional</span></span>
        <select name="quote_fragment_id" data-quote>
          <option value="">None</option>
          {quotes.map((q) => <option value={q.id}>{q.label}</option>)}
        </select>
        <span class="f__h">It leads the open set, above your description.</span>
      </label>

      {
        /* ⚠ A MINI EDITOR, NOT A TEXTAREA (plan 42 · §4.D.5). `MusicSets.astro`
          already renders this field through `renderMarkdown`, so the PUBLIC
          page has always supported bold and italic and only the editor refused
          — the one prose field in the building where what you type and what
          ships were different languages.

          The same control the quote body, the song notes and the constellation
          description use, for the reason that last one gives: ADR 0006 chose
          WYSIWYG-storing-Markdown for essays and ADR 0018 retired "plain
          textarea, ever" for notes, and this was the last room those two
          rulings had not reached.

          ⚠ THE VALUE TRAVELS IN A HIDDEN INPUT — a contenteditable is not a
          form control, so `new FormData(form)` cannot see it. */
      }
      <div class="f mt-4">
        <span class="f__k">Description</span>
        <MiniEditor id="set-desc" wrapId="set-desc-wrap" size="max-h-[30vh] min-h-[4.5rem]" />
        <input type="hidden" name="description" data-desc-value />
      </div>

      {
        /* ⚠ A SWITCH, FORM-BOUND, AND THE LIST HAS THE OTHER ONE. This is the
          composer's shape exactly (plan 42 · §0): two states that are opposites
          read as a switch, where a `<select>` made you open a menu to learn
          that and put the current state behind it. The old page used a select,
          which the composer's own comment had already argued against in writing
          — six days earlier.

          IT SAVES WITH THE CARD rather than on flip, and the line beneath says
          so until you press Save. The list row's ⋯ menu is the click-is-commit
          half; two screens, one fact, which is the constellations shape Michael
          already lives with. Plan 41's finding was two controls for one fact on
          ONE screen.

          ⚠ THE CHECKBOX IS THE FIELD — no hidden mirror. Unchecked, a checkbox
          submits nothing, and `sets.setStatus` is the only writer anyway, so
          `set-sheet.ts` reads this and calls that. */
      }
      <div class="f mt-4">
        <span class="f__k">Visibility</span>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-2.5">
          <input type="checkbox" id="set-status" class="toggle toggle-sm toggle-primary" data-status />
          <label id="set-status-word" for="set-status" class="cursor-pointer font-sans text-sm">Draft</label>
        </div>
        <p class="f__h" data-status-hint>Only you can see it. It joins the music page when you press Save.</p>
      </div>

      <label class="f mt-4">
        <span class="f__k">Address <span>— the URL</span></span>
        <input type="text" name="slug" placeholder="auto" spellcheck="false" autocapitalize="off" />
        <span class="f__h">Leave blank to follow the title.</span>
      </label>
    </div>

    <div class="border-base-300 bg-base-200 flex shrink-0 items-center gap-2 border-t px-6 py-3">
      {
        /* The footer variant, and it is legal here for the reason `sheet.ts`
          states: a short record whose form does not scroll has no "end of the
          object" distinct from its footer. ⚠ The confirm carries the
          irreversibility, because `sets.remove` is a hard delete — argued at
          the action, and correct: a set is a title, a sentence and a link. */
      }
      <button type="button" class="btn btn-ghost btn-sm text-error gap-1 font-sans" data-delete hidden>
        <Icon name="ph:trash" /> Delete
      </button>
      <button type="button" data-close class="btn btn-ghost btn-sm ml-auto font-sans">Cancel</button>
      {
        /* Named, per the rule in `SongSheet.astro`: this room holds several
          savable sets, so the label carries the noun. */
      }
      <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary btn-sm font-sans normal-case" data-submit>Save set</button>
    </div>
  </form>
</dialog>

<script>
  import '../../scripts/set-sheet';
</script>
